<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter10_copy-constructors-copy-assignment-and-explicit">
    <title>Copy Constructors, Copy Assignment, and <c>explicit</c></title>

    <p>
        When a class object is copied, C++ must decide how to initialize or
        update the destination object. Two operations are especially important:
        a <term>copy constructor</term> creates a new object from an existing
        object, while a <term>copy-assignment operation</term> updates an object
        that already exists.
    </p>

    <p>
        C++ can generate ordinary memberwise copying automatically. That default
        behavior is useful for many classes, but it can be unsafe when a class
        owns a raw pointer. We will first learn the two copying operations and
        then see why resource-owning classes need special care.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter10_copy-constructor">
        <title>The Copy Constructor</title>

        <p>
            A copy constructor is a constructor whose parameter is a reference to
            an object of the same class. The usual form is:
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_chapter10_copy_constructor_syntax"
                 language="cpp">
            <code><![CDATA[
class Score {
public:
    Score(const Score& other) {
        // Copy the state from other.
    }
};
            ]]></code>
        </program>

        <p>
            The ampersand means that <c>other</c> is received by reference, so
            C++ does not make another copy just to pass the source object into
            the copy constructor. Without the ampersand, passing a
            <c>Score</c> by value would require another copy and could cause the
            copy constructor to call itself repeatedly. The <c>const</c>
            keyword allows the constructor to copy from a read-only source and
            promises that it will not modify that source. A copy constructor is
            used when a new object is initialized from an existing object.
        </p>

        <listing xml:id="listing_chapter10_copy_constructor">
            <title>Copy construction and copy assignment</title>
            <program xml:id="program_chapter10_copy_constructor"
                     language="cpp"
                     interactive="yes"
                     line-numbers="yes">
                <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

class Score {
private:
    int value;

public:
    explicit Score(int starting_value)
        : value{starting_value} {
    }

    int get_value() const {
        return value;
    }
};

int main() {
    Score original{90};
    Score copied{original};   // C++ supplies the copy constructor.

    Score assigned{0};
    assigned = original;

    std::cout << copied.get_value() << '\n';
    std::cout << assigned.get_value() << '\n';

    return 0;
}
                ]]></code>
            </program>
        </listing>

        <p>
            <c>Score copied{original};</c> creates a new object, so it uses the
            copy constructor. The statement <c>assigned = original;</c> changes
            an object that was already created, so it uses copy assignment. The
            compiler can provide a memberwise copy-assignment operation even when
            we do not write one ourselves.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter10_copy-assignment">
        <title>Copy Assignment Updates an Existing Object</title>

        <table xml:id="table_chapter10_copy_operations">
            <title>Copy construction and copy assignment</title>
            <tabular top="major" left="major" bottom="major" right="major">
                <col width="32%"/>
                <col width="32%"/>
                <col width="36%"/>
                <row header="yes">
                    <cell>Expression</cell>
                    <cell>Operation</cell>
                    <cell>Destination state</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><c>Score copy{original};</c></cell>
                    <cell>Copy construction</cell>
                    <cell><c>copy</c> is a new object.</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><c>Score copy = original;</c></cell>
                    <cell>Copy initialization</cell>
                    <cell><c>copy</c> is a new object.</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><c>copy = original;</c></cell>
                    <cell>Copy assignment</cell>
                    <cell><c>copy</c> already existed.</cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
            The assignment statement does not call a constructor for the
            destination. The destination was already initialized. It instead
            copies the source object's current state into the existing object.
            The assignment syntax will be studied more broadly with operator
            overloading later in this chapter.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter10_explicit-and-copy-initialization">
        <title><c>explicit</c> and Copy Initialization</title>

        <p>
            The <c>explicit</c> keyword prevents a constructor with one argument
            from being used for an unintended implicit conversion. It does not
            prevent ordinary copying of an object from another object of the same
            type.
        </p>

        <listing xml:id="listing_chapter10_explicit_constructor_copy">
            <title>Using an <c>explicit</c> constructor and copying an object</title>
            <program xml:id="program_chapter10_explicit_constructor_copy"
                     language="cpp"
                     interactive="yes"
                     line-numbers="yes">
                <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

class Score {
private:
    int value;

public:
    explicit Score(int starting_value)
        : value{starting_value} {
    }

    int get_value() const {
        return value;
    }
};

int main() {
    Score original{90};
    Score copied = original;  // Copying an existing Score is permitted.

    // Score invalid = 90;    // Error: implicit conversion is prevented.
    Score valid{90};          // Direct-list initialization is permitted.

    std::cout << copied.get_value() << '\n';
    std::cout << valid.get_value() << '\n';

    return 0;
}
                ]]></code>
            </program>
        </listing>

        <p>
            The expression <c>Score copied = original;</c> initializes a new
            <c>Score</c> from an existing <c>Score</c>, so the copy constructor
            is appropriate. The commented statement would ask C++ to convert an
            <c>int</c> into a <c>Score</c> implicitly, which the
            <c>explicit</c> constructor prevents.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter10_copy-memberwise-pointer-risk">
        <title>Memberwise Copying and Raw Pointer Risk</title>

        <p>
            The compiler-generated copy operations copy each data member. For
            ordinary values such as integers and strings, this is often exactly
            what we want. For a raw pointer, however, memberwise copying copies
            the address rather than the dynamically allocated object.
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_chapter10_memberwise_pointer_risk"
                 language="cpp">
            <code><![CDATA[
class ScoreHolder {
private:
    int* score_pointer;

public:
    ScoreHolder(int starting_score)
        : score_pointer{new int{starting_score}} {
    }

    ~ScoreHolder() {
        delete score_pointer;
    }
};

// A compiler-generated copy can make two objects contain
// the same score_pointer address.
            ]]></code>
        </program>

        <p>
            If two objects contain the same owning pointer, both destructors may
            try to release the same allocation. This can cause undefined behavior.
            This address-only duplication is called a <term>shallow copy</term>:
            the pointer value is copied, but the dynamically allocated object is
            not duplicated. A <term>deep copy</term> would allocate separate
            storage for the second object and copy the pointed-to value into that
            new storage.
        </p>

        <p>
            A class that owns a raw resource commonly needs a destructor, a copy
            constructor, and a copy-assignment operation that coordinate with one
            another. This is the Rule of Three, which we will develop fully in
            Pointers and Dynamic Memory, Part 2.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter10_copy-codelens">
        <title>Tracing Copy Construction</title>

        <p>
            The visualization steps through creation of an original object and a
            copied object to show how data is replicated in memory. The two
            objects contain equal values, but they are separate objects.
        </p>

        <interactive xml:id="interactive_chapter10_copy_constructor"
                     iframe="https://pythontutor.com/iframe-embed.html#code=%23include%20%3Ciostream%3E%0A%0Aclass%20Score%20%7B%0Apublic%3A%0A%20%20%20%20int%20value%3B%0A%0A%20%20%20%20Score%28int%20starting_value%29%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%3A%20value%7Bstarting_value%7D%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20%7D%0A%0A%20%20%20%20Score%28const%20Score%26%20other%29%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%3A%20value%7Bother.value%7D%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20%7D%0A%7D%3B%0A%0Aint%20main%28%29%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20Score%20original%7B90%7D%3B%0A%20%20%20%20Score%20copied%7Boriginal%7D%3B%0A%0A%20%20%20%20copied.value%20%3D%2095%3B%0A%20%20%20%20std%3A%3Acout%20%3C%3C%20original.value%20%3C%3C%20%27%5Cn%27%3B%0A%20%20%20%20std%3A%3Acout%20%3C%3C%20copied.value%20%3C%3C%20%27%5Cn%27%3B%0A%0A%20%20%20%20return%200%3B%0A%7D&amp;codeDivHeight=560&amp;codeDivWidth=450&amp;cppShowMemAddrs=true&amp;mode=display&amp;curInstr=0&amp;origin=opt-frontend.js&amp;py=cpp"
                     width="100%"
                     aspect="4:3">
        </interactive>
    </subsection>

    <exercise label="chapter10_copy_exercise_difference">
        <statement>
            <p>What is the difference between <c>Score copy{original};</c> and <c>copy = original;</c>?</p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p>The first creates a new object; the second updates an existing object.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Copy construction begins a new lifetime, while assignment changes an existing object.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>They always call exactly the same constructor.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Assignment does not construct the already existing destination.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>The first changes the original; the second creates a new object.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>The destination of each expression determines the operation.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>The first works only for pointers; the second works only for structures.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Both operations can apply to user-defined class objects.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter10_copy_exercise_signature">
        <statement>
            <p>Which declaration has the usual form of a copy constructor for <c>Score</c>?</p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p><c>Score(const Score&amp; other);</c></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>A copy constructor receives a const reference to an object of the same class.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p><c>Score(int other);</c></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>This is a converting or ordinary value constructor, not a copy constructor.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p><c>void Score(Score other);</c></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Constructors have no return type, and passing by value would require another copy.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p><c>Score* Score(const Score&amp; other);</c></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>A constructor does not declare a return type or return a pointer.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter10_copy_parsons_operations" adaptive="yes" indentation="hide">
        <statement>
            <p>Arrange the statements to demonstrate copy construction followed by copy assignment.</p>
        </statement>
        <blocks>
            <block order="1"><cline>Score original{90};</cline></block>
            <block order="2"><cline>Score copied{original};</cline></block>
            <block order="3"><cline>Score assigned{0};</cline></block>
            <block order="4"><cline>assigned = original;</cline></block>
        </blocks>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter10_copy_exercise_explicit">
        <statement>
            <p>What does <c>explicit Score(int starting_value)</c> prevent?</p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p>Implicit conversion of an <c>int</c> into a <c>Score</c></p></statement>
                <feedback><p><c>explicit</c> requires direct initialization such as <c>Score score{90};</c>.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>Copying one <c>Score</c> object into another</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Ordinary copying between <c>Score</c> objects remains allowed.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>Calling every member function</p></statement>
                <feedback><p><c>explicit</c> affects constructor conversions, not member-function calls.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>Creating a <c>Score</c> with braces</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Direct-list initialization with braces is permitted.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter10_copy_exercise_pointer_risk">
        <statement>
            <p>Why can compiler-generated memberwise copying be dangerous for a class that owns a raw pointer?</p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p>It can copy the address, causing two objects to release the same allocation.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Copying a pointer value does not copy the dynamically allocated object.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>It always changes the pointer into a reference.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Memberwise copying preserves the pointer type and copies its address value.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>It prevents the destructor from running.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>The destructor still runs, which is part of the danger when ownership is duplicated.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>It automatically converts the raw pointer to a smart pointer.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Compiler-generated copying does not change the pointer type.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

</section>
